name o1-10-mole-cricket-1
timeout 900

# CAMPAIGN O1, SORTIE 10 OF 10 - THE WHOLE BATTLE, AND THE CAMPAIGN'S ACTUAL ANSWER.
# THIS FILE IS THE TERMINUS OF THE CARRY CHAIN. It flies over the belt sortie 08 left standing, with
# the aircraft sortie 08 left alive, on the magazines sortie 08 left loaded. Its control is not another
# mission: it is ITSELF, run standalone with no carried state, which is what
# `tools/fb_campaign_verify.py replay` produces for every step of a campaign. The difference between
# the two runs of this one file IS the campaign's attrition, isolated.
#
# THE ARENA, and every coordinate in it is [SET]. The Bekaa runs NNE-SSW between the Lebanon and the
# Anti-Lebanon; FlightBox flies it as a parallel at 33.90 N because it has NO TERRAIN MASKING (gap C4),
# so the two ridges that are the valley's whole geography do not exist in this tree and a mission that
# pretended otherwise would be lying with coordinates. The campaign is flown with `--elev const`, i.e.
# on a 0 m datum: the real valley floor is ~900 m, so every altitude here is height above the DECLARED
# datum and not above the real Bekaa. Rayak is 33.85 N 35.99 E and Damascus/Mezzeh 33.48 N 36.22 E [T4];
# the SAM belt of this campaign stands at 36.20-36.40 E, i.e. the eastern group, between the two.
#
# THE SUBSTITUTION, AND ITS DIRECTION — this is the binding disclosure of doc/campaigns/o1-bekaa-1982.md
# and it is repeated in every file of this campaign because a reader who skips it will read the result
# backwards. The MiG-29 was NOT at Bekaa: it entered Soviet service in 1983, and on 9 June 1982 the
# Syrian side flew MiG-21, MiG-23 and Su-20 [T4]. Those aircraft had "only nose and tail alert radar
# systems and no side warnings or look-up and look-down systems" [T4]. FlightBox's MiG-29 has the SPO-15
# with eight azimuth channels, a look-down-capable N019 and an IRST. THE SUBSTITUTION THEREFORE MAKES THE
# DEFENDER STRONGER THAN HISTORY. A FlightBox Bekaa that still ends in a rout says something STRONGER
# than 1982 did; one that ends well for Red says NOTHING about 1982 and must not be read as if it did.
#
# WHY RED NEVER FLIES OVER ITS OWN BELT, AND IT IS A GAP AND NOT A CHOICE. A FlightBox SAM site has no
# IFF interrogator (`modules/ground/FBSiteModule`: "a battery has no IFF interrogator in this tree") and
# a radar contact carries no identity by construction, so a battery engages the nearest firm track in its
# envelope WHOEVER IT BELONGS TO. Measured on this campaign's own first layout: with the Syrian CAP at
# 8 000 m over the belt, `swsa2` put three V-750 into its own fighters inside 7 s. Every mission of O1 is
# therefore laid out so that no Red aircraft is ever inside a friendly envelope — which DELETES the
# anchor's own Syrian tactic ("do what they can, then run back for cover" inside the protective missile
# envelope [T4]). That is stated here rather than hidden in a gap table: the campaign cannot fly the one
# thing the Syrians actually did.
#
# NO AIRCRAFT IN THIS SORTIE DECLARES `objective survive`, AND NO FIGHTER FLIES IN IT AT ALL. Both are
# consequences of one runner property rather than of doctrine: `FBMissionRunner` ENDS THE WHOLE RUN at
# the first flight-monitor K.O. and at the first failed `survive` (`FirstFlightKo` / `FirstDecidingFailure`).
# MEASURED on this campaign's own first layout of this sortie: with four MiG-29 and two escorts in the
# file, the fighter engagement produced its first combat-ineffective jet at t = 169.9 s and that jet hit
# the ground at t = 237.0 s - so every strike sortie ended at t = 237.0 with the strikers still 130 km
# short of the belt and the belt with nothing to shoot at. Putting a fighter duel and a SEAD run in one
# file therefore does not measure the SEAD run; it measures the duel twice. The fighters have their own
# seven sorties, and sorties 08 and 09 are the SEAD pair the operation was actually named after.
# The loss of an aircraft is carried by the campaign regardless of any objective (the carry reads the
# roster's CombatEffective bit), so dropping the line costs the campaign nothing.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION - THE YARDSTICK. With every lever set to the best value measured in sorties
# 01 to 09, what is the RESIDUE? doc/campaigns/o1-bekaa-1982.md section Knowledge 3 defines it: with the
# Red doctrine vector at its measured optimum and Blue flown to its own, whatever outcome is LEFT is the
# structural part of Bekaa - the part no decision on either side removes. If the residue is small,
# FlightBox is saying 1982 was a doctrine and architecture defeat that better decisions could have
# changed. If it is large, FlightBox is saying the Syrian force was outmatched in materiel before
# anybody decided anything. Either answer is publishable; refusing to compute it is not.
#
# WHAT "BEST" MEANS HERE, and every one of them is a measurement from this campaign and not a taste:
#   Red doctrine   `pilot_lock_nm 16` + `pilot_shot_rtr 1.4` - sortie 07, the ONLY lever of the seven
#                  that inverted the baseline (2 Red mission-killed / 0 Blue became 0 / 2).
#   Red control    the full four-call GCI brief - sortie 05 measured its absence as ZERO contacts and
#                  ZERO shots on the entry geometry a controller exists for.
#   Blue jamming   `set jam_comm_m 90000` - sortie 09 measured the netted belt at 5 launches and the
#                  jammed one at 0, with no firm track at all.
#   Blue package   escorts high and forward, strikers already past the CAP line and 32 km from their
#                  aim points, so the SEAD run and the fighter engagement resolve in PARALLEL and 90 km
#                  apart. That is not a convenience: `FBMissionRunner` ends the whole run at the first
#                  flight-monitor K.O., so a file in which the fighter fight must FINISH before the
#                  strike begins measures the fighter fight and nothing else (MEASURED: t = 237.0 s).
#
# THE FORCE: eight MiG-29 in two flights of four against four F-16 escorts and four F-16 strikers, plus
# the EF-111 and the belt. The Israeli order of battle at Bekaa was 96 aircraft against roughly 100
# Syrian [T4]; this is that ratio at 1:12 scale, and the scale is declared rather than implied.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint 36c498ac10e1ab79 as campaign step 10, `--elev const`): WITH EVERY LEVER AT ITS
# MEASURED BEST, RED LOSES NOTHING AND BLUE LOSES TWO. Thirteen R-27R away from eight MiGs against two
# AIM-120 from four escorts; seven detonations; `eagle2` mission-killed by a 2.03 m arrival at
# t = 168.5 and `eagle3` by 2.56 / 3.63 / 3.25 m at t = 178.7-181.0. Zero Red losses. The run ends at
# t = 203.1 s on `eagle2`'s ground contact, which is `FirstFlightKo` and not the clock.
#
# THE CARRY, AND WHAT IT WAS WORTH. Five `campaign CARRY` lines on entry: `bksa6a`, `bksa6b` and
# `bksa3` dropped (destroyed in sortie 08 - two of them by their own rounds), and one `set store mk82`
# line dropped from each of `bolt1` and `bolt2`. Against the same file run STANDALONE with no carried
# state, the ONE measured difference is the belt: `site LAUNCH` 5 -> 3, and the two self-destructions
# 2 -> 0 because the batteries that would have killed themselves were already dead. EVERY AIR NUMBER IS
# UNCHANGED - same 13 shots, same 2 shots, same two escorts lost, same end tick. The attrition arc is
# visible, it is auditable line by line, and IT MOVED NO OUTCOME.
#
# THE RESIDUE, WHICH IS THE CAMPAIGN'S ACTUAL ANSWER, IS THEREFORE NOT A RESIDUE OF BEKAA. With the
# best doctrine measured in 01-07 the defender wins outright; but the doctrine that produced that win
# is `pilot_shot_rtr 1.4`, a launch discipline available to BOTH sides and given to only one, and the
# defender is an aircraft that postdates the battle by a year and carries a radar the real one did not
# have. What the number says is that in THIS tree, on THIS geometry, the whole outcome sits inside a
# 3.5-second launch decision - and nothing about the ground, the net, the jamming or the warning
# receiver reaches it at all.
#
# READING RULE: the exit code is not the verdict. Read, in this order: (1) the `campaign CARRY` lines in
# this step's own events.log - they ARE the attrition arc; (2) `site LAUNCH` and `net LOST` on the belt;
# (3) `objective kill unit` on the four strikers; (4) the per-jet `eng_*` debriefing and the DETONATION
# lines; (5) the ATTRITION line of the campaign report. exit 2 would be a defect.

wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

zone flak    33.90000 36.30000  9000    0  2500   # under the SAMs, above nothing
zone sa6belt 33.90000 36.30000 24000  100  7000   # the mobile layer
zone sa2belt 33.90000 36.30000 30000  450 25000   # the strategic layer

net iads
  control bkp18                                # the node: a search set and a radio
  link radio 60000 mast 8                       # [SET] 8 m masts -> 23.3 km of horizon. RADIO, because
  period 4.0                                    # a buried cable is unjammable and the anchor's decisive
  hold 3                                        # mechanism was jamming the RADIO net
  wcs free                                      # what the node transmits
  member bkp18
  member bksa6a autonomy hold
  member bksa6b autonomy hold
  member bksa2  autonomy hold
  member bksa3  autonomy hold

unit hawk1
  team hostile
  flight syr10a 1
  module mig29
  spawn 33.88334 35.75000 2000 270.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_mode rad                           # the wide non-locking search pattern
  set n019_range_nm 27                        # the set's own documented search reach
  set n019_emission illum                     # LOUD from t=0: see the header on why this is the baseline
  set rwr on
  set brief_sort left                         # the briefed contract; this jet has no cooperative channel
  set store 3 r27r                            # FIRING ORDER, not a pylon map (doc/duels.md D4)
  set store 4 r27r
  set store 5 r73
  set store 6 r73
  set store 1 r73
  set store 2 r73
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 30                           # BVP-30-26, 30/30
  set cmds_flare 30
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  set pilot_lock_nm 16                        # THE BEST DOCTRINE MEASURED IN 01-07: sortie 07
  set pilot_shot_rtr 1.4                      # is the only lever that inverted the baseline
  set brief_gci 20 270 97 9
  set brief_gci 80 270 69 9
  set brief_gci 140 270 42 9
  set brief_gci 200 270 14 9
  wp 33.90000 34.90000 2000 450
  objective kill team friendly

